Script — Rename Files to Uppercase
Script
#!/bin/bash
# Loop through all arguments
for file in "$@"
do
  # Convert filename to uppercase and rename
  mv "$file" "$(echo "$file" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]')"
done




Line-by-Line Explanation
Line 1 — #!/bin/bash
This is called the shebang.
It tells Linux to use the Bash shell to execute the script.
Line 2 — # Loop through all arguments
This is a comment.
Comments are ignored by Bash.
It describes what the following code does.
Line 3 — for file in "$@"
This starts a for loop.
for → starts the loop.
file → variable that stores one filename at a time.
"$@" → represents all command-line arguments supplied to the script.
For example:
./rename.sh notes.txt report.txt data.txt


The loop processes notes.txt, report.txt, and data.txt one by one.
Line 4 — do
Marks the beginning of the commands that will be executed for each argument.
Everything between do and done belongs to the loop.
Line 5 — # Convert filename to uppercase and rename
This is another comment.
It explains that the next command will convert the filename to uppercase and rename it.
Line 6 — mv "$file" "$(echo "$file" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]')"
This is the main command of the script. It uses mv to rename the file.
Component
Meaning
mv
Moves or renames files. Example: mv old.txt new.txt renames old.txt to new.txt.
"$file"
Represents the original filename. If file is notes.txt, then "$file" represents notes.txt.
$(...)
Command substitution — executes the command inside the parentheses and uses its output as a value. Here, it generates the new filename.
echo "$file"
Displays the current filename, e.g. notes.txt.
|
A pipe — sends the output of one command to another. echo "$file" | tr ... takes the filename and sends it to tr.
tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'
Converts lowercase characters to uppercase, e.g. notes.txt becomes NOTES.TXT.


Put together, $(echo "$file" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]') produces the new uppercase filename, and the complete command effectively becomes:
mv "notes.txt" "NOTES.TXT"


So the file is renamed.
Line 7 — done
Marks the end of the for loop.
Bash then takes the next command-line argument and repeats the process.
How to Run the Script
Step 1: Create the script
nano rename.sh


Enter the script and save it.
Step 2: Give execute permission
chmod +x rename.sh


Step 3: Create some test files
touch notes.txt report.txt hello.txt


Step 4: Run the script
./rename.sh notes.txt report.txt hello.txt

The files will be renamed to:
NOTES.TXT
REPORT.TXT
HELLO.TXT




Important Point
This script changes only the filenames, not the contents of the files.
For example, notes.txt containing "hello linux" will become NOTES.TXT — but its contents will remain "hello linux".
